  and validation live in exactly one table, so adding a preference is a one-line registry entry. The [38;5;153mv[39m field gives you a
  migration hook before you need it. Costs you: a two-tab race where both write different preferences means
  last-writer-wins clobbers the other field. For per-device UI settings I think that's an acceptable loss; it's the
  classic objection to blob storage and it's real.

  [1mB. One namespaced key per preference[0m

  [38;5;153mwebapp.pref.rememberedUsername[39m, [38;5;153mwebapp.pref.theme[39m, written independently.

  Buys you: no cross-field clobber — two tabs writing different preferences both survive. Per-key migration is
  independent. Costs you: no atomic multi-field update, you need a key registry anyway to enumerate and clear
  preferences, and schema versioning gets awkward (a version per key, or a side-car version key that reintroduces the
  coupling you were avoiding). You pay real structural complexity for a concurrency guarantee that a single-user
  settings panel rarely needs.

  [1mC. Hydrate-once in-memory store with an injected storage adapter[0m

  Parse localStorage once at load into an in-memory object; reads hit memory only, writes update memory and flush
  through. The storage backend is a constructor parameter defaulting to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  Buys you: reads are free, and the injection seam makes Node testing trivial — pass a [38;5;153mMap[39m-backed fake, no jsdom, no
  dependency. Costs you: the in-memory copy goes stale the moment another tab writes, so you'd want a [38;5;153mstorage[39m event
  listener to resync, which is a real chunk of the complexity back. It also front-loads a performance concern that
  doesn't exist — we're talking about two values read on page load.

  [1mMy recommendation: A, with C's injected-storage seam grafted on.[0m The blob's clobber risk is the weakest objection of
  the three because these are per-device UI settings a single user changes deliberately, not concurrent shared state.
  But C is right about testability, and that part is free: making the storage backend an injectable parameter costs one
  constructor argument and buys dependency-free unit tests in Node's built-in runner. B's advantage only pays off under
  genuine multi-tab write contention, which isn't this app. Taking A now doesn't foreclose B — the registry makes the
  on-disk layout an implementation detail behind a stable [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m API.

  Does that read right before I go into the design sections?

